// Ages - Introduction
// This program demonstrates basic operations on an array of ages.
// It calculates the average age, as well as finds the minimum and maximum ages.

function main() {
    // Initialize an array of ages
    ages = [25, 30, 45, 22, 18, 60]
    
    // Variables to store the sum, minimum, and maximum ages
    sum = 0
    minAge = ages[0]
    maxAge = ages[0]
    
    // Iterate over each age in the array
    for age in ages {
        // Update the sum
        sum = sum + age
        
        // Check for a new minimum age
        if (age < minAge) {
            minAge = age
        }
        
        // Check for a new maximum age
        if (age > maxAge) {
            maxAge = age
        }
    }
    
    // Calculate the average age
    averageAge = sum / length(ages)
    
    // Print the results
    print("Average Age:", averageAge)
    print("Minimum Age:", minAge)
    print("Maximum Age:", maxAge)
}

// Execute the main function
main()


Given an array of integers representing the ages of individuals, determine the age gap by computing the difference between the maximum age and the minimum age in the array. This simple yet common problem is used to assess numerical reasoning and array processing skills.

Constraints:

The number of ages (n) can range from 1 to 10^5.
Each age is an integer in the range [0, 150].
The input array may be unsorted.
Section 2 — Intuition
The crucial observation is that the maximum possible age difference is simply the difference between the oldest person (maximum value) and the youngest person (minimum value) in the array. A straightforward single pass through the list, maintaining both minimum and maximum values, ensures an optimal solution in linear time. This “aha moment” lies in reducing the problem from examining every possible pair (brute force) to just one traversal of the data.

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
A naive solution is to compute the difference for every pair of ages in the array, and then determine the maximum difference found. This involves a double loop through all elements.

Code (C++)
Copy
cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Example input: list of ages
    vector<int> ages = {23, 45, 19, 67, 34};
    int n = ages.size();
    
    int maxDiff = 0;
    
    // Brute force: check difference for every pair
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            int diff = abs(ages[i] - ages[j]);
            if (diff > maxDiff) {
                maxDiff = diff;
            }
        }
    }
    
    cout << "Age gap is: " << maxDiff << endl;
    return 0;
}
Time Complexity: O(n²) – every pair of ages is compared.
Space Complexity: O(1) – only a few extra variables are used.
Section 4 — Approach 2: Optimal Solution
A more efficient solution involves a single traversal:

Initialize two variables: minAge to a large value and maxAge to a small value.
Traverse the array and update minAge when a smaller age is found.
Update maxAge when a larger age is encountered.
Compute the difference as maxAge - minAge.
Code (C++)
