Section 6 — Detailed Dry Run
Consider the array: [23, 45, 19, 67, 34]

Index	Age	minAge Update	maxAge Update	minAge (after)	maxAge (after)
0	23	minAge = 23 (from ∞)	maxAge = 23 (from -∞)	23	23
1	45	No update	maxAge = 45 (45 > 23)	23	45
2	19	minAge = 19 (19 < 23)	No update	19	45
3	67	No update	maxAge = 67 (67 > 45)	19	67
4	34	No update	No update	19	67
Final age gap: 67 - 19 = 48

Section 7 — Edge Cases
Empty Array:
Input: []
Expected Output: May require a special message or error handling (e.g., "No ages provided").

Single Element:
Input: [25]
Expected Output: 0 (no difference).

All Same Elements:
Input: [30, 30, 30]
Expected Output: 0 (max and min are equal).

Negative Ages (if allowed by context):
Input: [-10, -5, -15]
Expected Output: Difference between maximum (-5) and minimum (-15) → 10.

Array with two elements:
Input: [40, 60]
Expected Output: 20.

Section 8 — Common Mistakes
Assuming Non-empty Input:
Not handling the empty array scenario can lead to runtime errors. Always check the input length first.

Using Wrong Initial Values:
Initializing minAge and maxAge with inappropriate values (e.g., 0) might fail if all ages are above or below 0. Use the maximum and minimum possible integers.

Overcomplicating the Problem:
Using nested loops unnecessarily beyond a simple single pass increases time complexity from O(n) to O(n²).

Ignoring Edge Cases:
Failing to consider cases with a single element or all elements being the same can lead to incorrect conclusions.

Not Validating Input Type:
Be sure to confirm that inputs are integers and within the valid range. Validation helps avoid unexpected errors.

